+# Web Push — prepared, not deployed
+
+This changes only PO. Production website, Auth settings, data and Edge Functions have not been modified. Do not merge into an auto-deploy branch until deployment is approved.
+
+## User flow
+
+Open the bell → แจ้งเตือนบนเครื่องนี้ → เปิดแจ้งเตือน → allow the browser/OS prompt. Normally once per browser/device. Reopening the website with an existing subscription does not prompt again. Signing out or switching account turns off the old account's subscription; enable it for the next account. Each device has a separate subscription. Clearing website data, removing the installed web app, revoking permission, expired provider subscriptions or rotating VAPID keys may require enabling again.
+
+For iOS/iPadOS 16.4+, add the website to Home Screen and launch that icon. The manifest marks this as a standalone web app. Android and desktop use supported browsers over HTTPS. Sounds/banners depend on system notification settings, mute/Focus; the website cannot force a sound. This implementation has not yet been verified on a real iPhone or Android device.
+
+## Event ownership
+
+Push is queued AFTER INSERT into the existing `public.notifications`, within the same database transaction. Failed or rolled-back PO actions enqueue nothing. Existing business RPC idempotency prevents duplicate event rows. Existing in-app notifications and read marking remain unchanged. No permission prompt occurs on page load.
+
+| Committed action currently present | Recipients |
+|---|---|
+| Create PO | Active owners |
+| Approve / reject | PO creator |
+| Receive goods | Active owners |
+| Close PO | PO creator |
+
+Return for revision, edit request/unlock and resubmit are not present in the reviewed main source. When those workflows are implemented, they must insert a new canonical notification for the proper recipient. Transport then queues it automatically. Do not claim those business flows exist because the Push transport exists. Existing notification action constraints/unique key must be reviewed for repeated revision rounds.
+
+The lock-screen message is generic: มีการอัปเดตใบ PO แตะเพื่อดูรายละเอียด. No product, vendor, price, reason, email or username is sent. Clicking opens the PO by UUID and the usual login/RLS checks still apply. No service-worker caches are created. IndexedDB contains only the opaque current subscription binding ID, not credentials or PO content.
+
+## Security and delivery
+
+Client registration RPCs verify an active profile and auth.uid(); callers cannot select a recipient. Endpoint/key tables have RLS and no direct anon/authenticated grants. The queue worker RPCs are executable only by service_role. The internal trigger function lives in a non-exposed schema. Only known Apple/Google/Mozilla/Microsoft HTTPS provider endpoints are accepted; URL length and subscription count are bounded. One endpoint cannot be transferred to another account. Logout clears local binding before provider unsubscription/server removal; late sync cannot reactivate it.
+
+Worker endpoint requires a dedicated `x-push-secret`, has no caller payload/recipient API and keeps service/VAPID private keys server-side. Jobs use SKIP LOCKED plus leases and token-checked acknowledgements. Provider 404/410 removes expired subscriptions; network/429/5xx retries with bounded backoff (5 attempts). A matching notification tag reduces duplicates, but delivery is at-least-once: uncertain network acknowledgements can still cause duplicates. OS delivery is not guaranteed and Push is supplementary to the in-app inbox. Queue jobs expire after a day; this does not delete business notifications or PO records.
+
+## Release steps — only after deployment approval
+
+1. Recheck main, apply this feature without overwriting newer changes, and run all tests. Check remote migration versions and the SQL dry run. Apply `20261006071609_web_push_notifications.sql` to an isolated Supabase staging project first. Check advisors, RPC grants and RLS before production.
+2. Generate one stable VAPID key pair privately. Store `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`, `VAPID_SUBJECT` and a random high-entropy `PUSH_WORKER_SECRET` as Edge Function secrets. Subject must be a real `mailto:` or HTTPS contact; never use localhost. Only the public key goes into `dist/config.js` as `vapidPublicKey`. Do not commit private keys or secret values. Do not create a new VAPID pair on each deploy.
+3. Run Deno check and generate the function's deno.lock in an environment with npm registry access before release. The import is pinned to web-push 3.6.7. The local Deno dependency check was blocked by registry connection refusal, so native Edge runtime compatibility is still a release gate. Then deploy `push-worker` with `verify_jwt=false`; the handler checks the scheduler secret. Supabase provides the service role environment internally. Confirm unauthorized calls return 401 before testing authorized jobs.
+4. Enable pg_cron and pg_net, store scheduler secret in Vault as `po_push_worker_secret`, and create the job below. This is an operator template, not an automatically applied migration. Keep Vault access restricted. Review cron.job_run_details and net._http_response retention in the capacity review.
+5. Deploy frontend assets together, including sw.js, manifest and both PNG icons. Service worker scope must be `/`; ensure sw.js is JavaScript and manifest uses application/manifest+json. Add cache revalidation for sw.js at the host. Do not change Auth recovery URLs or public signup for this feature.
+6. Test on isolated staging: each event above; correct recipient; foreground/background/closed app; iOS installed app; Android; desktop; denied permission; reload; logout/account switch; expired endpoint; offline retries; disabled account; tap with expired login. Then approve the production release. After release, verify actual delivery on the user's devices before marking live Push complete.
+
+Scheduler template (no secret literals):
+
+```sql
+select cron.schedule('po-push-worker', '* * * * *', $job$
+ select net.http_post(
+  url := 'https://rhkilsnuqdkzwlncjvkj.supabase.co/functions/v1/push-worker',
+  headers := jsonb_build_object('Content-Type','application/json',
+   'x-push-secret',(select decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets where name='po_push_worker_secret')),
+  body := '{}'::jsonb,
+  timeout_milliseconds := 120000
+ );
+$job$);
+```
+
+Use the staging project URL for staging. Production template is PO-specific. Expected dispatch starts with the next scheduled minute; large bursts/retries can take longer. Scheduler activation is part of deployment and has not been performed.
+
+## Rollback
+
+Unschedule `po-push-worker`, disable Push controls by reverting the public key/frontend release and leave existing PO tables/data intact. Preserve the private VAPID key for a later resume. Existing canonical in-app notifications still work. Do not DROP business tables or restore production to test rollback.
+
+## References
+
+- https://webkit.org/blog/13878/web-push-for-web-apps-on-ios-and-ipados/
+- https://supabase.com/docs/guides/functions/schedule-functions
+- https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/vault
+- https://github.com/web-push-libs/web-push
